    "body": "## Summary\n\nAdds **ADR-026: CSS Variable Resolution** — `:root` selector support + `var()` pre-resolution using CSS-spec fallback semantics. Second of a four-ADR arc (025→026→027→028) closing the parser gap that leaves Wikipedia's stylesheet partially un-applied.\n\n**Documentation only.** Implementation (3 commits) lands in a follow-up PR.\n\n## The inverted distribution finding\n\nMeasured on Wikipedia's CSS (2026-10-25):\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `var()` calls in CSS | 550 |\n| Variables defined in `:root` | **6** |\n| `var()` calls with fallback | **516 (93.8%)** |\n| `var()` calls referencing an undefined variable | **542 (98.5%)** |\n\n**98.5% undefined looks catastrophic.** But CSS spec says `var(--undefined, fallback)` resolves to the **fallback**. And the fallback distribution reveals the real story:\n\n| Fallback type | Count | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Color | **316** | `#202122`, `#36c`, `transparent` |\n| Length | **166** | `1rem`, `0.875rem`, `180px` |\n| Keyword | 17 | (inherit/initial/unset — unsupported) |\n| Other | 17 | (complex expressions) |\n\n**93% of fallbacks are values `parse_color` and ADR-013's length parser handle natively.** Effective resolution rate: **92.4% of `var()` calls** resolve to usable CSS values.\n\n**Top variable usages** (all undefined, all with usable fallbacks):\n\n| Variable | Uses | Fallback |\n|---|---|---|\n| `--font-size-medium` | 135 | `1rem` |\n| `--color-base` | 27 | `#202122` |\n| `--color-progressive` | 15 | `#36c` |\n| `--font-size-small` | 14 | `0.875rem` |\n| `--border-color-base` | 13 | `#a2a9b1` |\n\nAll five fallbacks are values existing parsers handle.\n\n## What the ADR specifies\n\n1. **`:root` selector**: new `SimpleKind::PseudoRoot`; matches the topmost element (no parent).\n2. **`Stylesheet::variables` field**: `unordered_map<string, string>`, populated during `parse()` from `:root` declarations whose name starts with `--`.\n3. **`var()` pre-resolution pass**: after parse, walk every declaration and resolve `var(--x, Y)` → variable value if defined else Y; `var(--x)` → variable value if defined else DROP the declaration (per CSS computed-value-time semantics).\n4. **Single-pass only**: recursive variables deferred (1 case on Wikipedia, consumers fall back anyway).\n\n## Why parse-time resolution\n\nThree reasons, ordered by importance:\n\n1. **Performance**: one resolution per rule vs. per element-application. On Wikipedia (~2,000 elements), ~2,000x speedup.\n2. **Correctness**: matches CSS \"computed-value time\" semantics for a stylesheet with static variables.\n3. **Zero downstream changes**: `parse_color(style.get(\"color\"))` sees `#202122` instead of `var(--color-base, #202122)` — every consumer works unchanged.\n\n## The Meta-lesson-v2 caveat (built into the ADR)\n\n**92.4% resolution rate is necessary but not sufficient for visible improvement.**\n\nThe rules whose declarations contain `var()` must also target **properties the layout engine consumes** — `color`, `background-color`, `border-color`, `font-size`, length-based margins/paddings. If most `var()` usage is in `box-shadow`, `transition`, or `border-radius` (none of which we render), resolution changes nothing visible.\n\n**Commit 2 includes a \"consumed-property coverage\" measurement.** If coverage <30%, ADR-026's Implementation section will document the resolution-vs-visible-delta gap honestly. Same discipline that made ADR-022 → 023 → 024 → 025 work.\n\n## The four-ADR arc (explicit)\n\n| ADR | Unlocks | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| **025** | External CSS loading (article content styled) | ✅ merged |\n| **026** | `:root` + `var()` (~92% of var() calls resolve) | this PR |\n| **027** | `@media` blocks (72 blocks — layout rules) | next |\n| **028** | `:not()` + attribute operators (391+150 rules) | after |\n\nADR-025 delivered ~40-50% visible improvement (exceeded its 25-35% prediction because content rules concentrated in the parseable half). ADR-026 targets the next chunk: font sizes and text colors that are currently dropped from the cascade because their `var()` values fail to parse.\n\n## Reviewer checklist\n\n- [ ] **The inverted finding is correctly interpreted** — 98.5% undefined + 93.8% fallback coverage = 92.4% effective resolution. Comfortable with the reasoning? (My read: yes — CSS spec is unambiguous on fallback behavior; the fallback distribution confirms most fallbacks are usable.)\n- [ ] **Parse-time resolution vs. cascade-time** — tradeoff is \"dynamic variables deferred\" for \"2,000x speedup + zero downstream changes.\" Acceptable for v1?\n- [ ] **Single-pass resolution** — recursive variables deferred. Only 1 recursive case on Wikipedia. Comfortable?\n- [ ] **Dropping declarations with unresolved no-fallback `var()`** — 34 calls affected. This preserves correct cascade semantics (invalid-at-computed-value-time declarations don't participate). Agree?\n- [ ] **Meta-lesson-v2 caveat** — \"92.4% resolution ≠ 92.4% visible.\" The consumed-property coverage measurement is what decides. Comfortable with that commitment, or want the measurement done before merging this spec?\n\n## Deferred (enumerated in ADR)\n\n- Recursive `var()` (1 case on Wikipedia)\n- Element-scoped custom properties (`.theme-dark { --bg: black }`)\n- Dynamic variable cascade (`:hover`-changing variables — incompatible with parse-time resolution)\n- Keyword fallbacks (`inherit`/`initial`/`unset` — 17 cases, needs `parse_color`/length changes)\n- Merged UA+author variable maps (not needed until UA uses `:root`)\n- `env()` environment variables (not in Wikipedia's CSS)\n\n## Test plan\n\nNo code touched, so no test changes. Will run `cmake --build build && ./build/tests/honeybee_tests` after merge to confirm `main` still clean (288 cases, 1239 assertions, unchanged).\n\n## ADR\n\n`docs/decisions/026-css-variables.md` — status Accepted; implementation in follow-up PR.\n\nRefs #154\n",
